  "output": "I now have a complete picture. This is an early-stage Next.js console app (Drizzle/Postgres + Clerk), almost entirely Server Components with no client-side data fetching yet. Findings are proportionate to that — there's no N+1 query mess or leaked subscriptions yet, but there are a few real issues and several things worth fixing *before* the app grows (the codebase's own comments say TanStack Query polling is coming for run status).\n\n## 1. Latent N+1 pattern — `countSourcesBySide` (`lib/server/sources.ts:23-30`)\n\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();       // full SELECT, then Zod-parses every row\n  return { crm: all.filter(...).length, warehouse: ..., synthetic: ... };\n}\n```\n\nNot a network N+1 yet, but it's doing a full table scan + full Zod validation of every row just to produce three counts, and any caller of both `listSources()` and `countSourcesBySide()` on the same request re-runs the query twice (no request-level caching — see #3). Once `sources` grows, push the count into SQL:\n\n```ts\nimport { count } from \"drizzle-orm\";\n\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const rows = await getDb()\n    .select({ kind: sources.kind, n: count() })\n    .from(sources)\n    .groupBy(sources.kind);\n  // fold rows into { crm, warehouse, synthetic } via SOURCE_KIND_SIDE\n}\n```\n\nThe bigger risk is structural: `runs` → `entities` → `matches` → `conflicts` → `resolutions` is a 5-level FK chain (`lib/db/schema.ts:65-174`). When you write the conflicts/audit pages, the naive approach (fetch conflicts, then loop to fetch each match, then each resolution) will be a textbook N+1. Use Drizzle's relational queries or explicit `innerJoin`s to fetch it in one query up front — worth deciding this now since the schema and data-access pattern (`lib/server/sources.ts`) will be the template every future `lib/server/*.ts` file copies.\n\n## 2. `getDb()` connection caching only works around dev HMR, not real pooling (`lib/db/index.ts:22-44`)\n\n```ts\nconst globalForDb = globalThis as unknown as { canonSql?: ...; canonDb?: ... };\n```\n\nThis is correct for avoiding connection leaks across HMR reloads in dev, and in most serverless Next deploys each isolate gets its own `globalThis` so this is fine. Just confirm the deploy target (Vercel Node runtime vs Edge, or long-lived container) — if this ever runs on the Edge runtime, `postgres-js` with a persistent TCP socket won't work at all and needs an HTTP-based driver instead. No change needed today, just flagging before it scales.\n\n## 3. No caching between `listSources()` calls in the same render\n\n`SourcesPage` (`app/(console)/sources/page.tsx:10`) calls `listSources()` once, but as soon as a second consumer needs it in the same request (e.g., a header badge showing count, or a layout-level check), you get a duplicate query — React's `cache()` isn't used anywhere. Wrap request-scoped reads:\n\n```ts\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\n\nexport const listSources = cache(async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n  return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n});\n```\n\nThis dedupes calls within a single request/render pass for free (no extra dependency), and costs nothing since `sources` is already request-time state via `connection()`.\n\n## 4. `requireReviewer()` double-fetches Clerk on every call (`lib/server/auth.ts:30-39`)\n\n```ts\nexport async function requireReviewer(): Promise<Reviewer> {\n  const { isAuthenticated, userId } = await auth();\n  ...\n  const user = await currentUser();   // second round trip for the same session\n  return { userId, label: reviewerLabel(user, userId) };\n}\n```\n\n`auth()` and `currentUser()` are two separate Clerk calls per invocation, and `getReviewer()` calls `auth()` and then `requireReviewer()`, which calls `auth()` again — three Clerk calls where one or two suffice. Clerk's `auth()`/`currentUser()` are already deduped per-request via Next's `cache()` internally in recent `@clerk/nextjs` versions, but confirm your version does this (check `@clerk/nextjs` in package.json) — if not, wrap this function in React's `cache()` the same way as `listSources`, since every protected page/layout will call `requireReviewer()`.\n\n## 5. React re-renders / client-component surface — currently fine, but one thing to fix now\n\nNone of the current client components (`nav-rail.tsx`, `landing-hero.tsx`, `pipeline-strip.tsx`, `step-list.tsx`, `sources-empty-state.tsx`) have unnecessary re-render problems — no lifted state, no prop-drilling of unstable objects, no missing memoization that matters at this scale (all render once, driven by route/props).\n\nOne real issue: **`NavRail` is a big client component that re-renders on every route change** (`components/console/nav-rail.tsx:24-82`), because `usePathname()` changes on navigation and the whole rail — including the `UserButton`/`useUser()` subtree — re-renders even though only the active `<Link>` classNames actually change. Since it's small today this isn't costly, but as you add child components under the rail, split the nav list into its own client component so `usePathname()` churn doesn't force Clerk's `UserButton` to re-render:\n\n```tsx\n// nav-rail.tsx (server-renderable shell)\n// nav-links.tsx (\"use client\", only usePathname + the <ul>)\n// user-panel.tsx (\"use client\", only useUser + UserButton + SignOutControl)\n```\n\n## 6. Memory leaks — none present, but the doc comments flag a coming risk\n\n`lib/server/sources.ts:8-9` explicitly says \"TanStack Query is reserved for run-status polling only\" — when that's built, the standard leak to avoid is a `setInterval`/`useQuery({refetchInterval})` polling hook that isn't cleaned up on unmount, or a poll that keeps running for a run that's already finished. When you implement it:\n- Use TanStack Query's `refetchInterval` returning `false` once `status` is terminal (`succeeded`/`failed`), not a manual `setInterval`.\n- Don't start polling in a component that can unmount mid-run without a cleanup — TanStack Query handles this automatically if you don't hand-roll intervals.\n\n## 7. Redundant computation — `source.config.objects.join(\", \")` re-run every render (`components/sources/source-table.tsx:29`)\n\nTrivial at current scale (a handful of rows, cheap join), not worth memoizing. Flagging only so it's not copied into a component with thousands of rows without a second thought — if `SourceTable` ever needs client-side sort/filter, memoize the derived label per row then.\n\n---\n\n**Bottom line:** the codebase is small enough that there's no live N+1 or leak yet. The actionable items are: (1) push `countSourcesBySide` into SQL and dedupe `listSources`/`requireReviewer` with `cache()` before more pages call them, (2) plan the multi-table conflict/resolution fetch as joins, not loops, before that page is built, and (3) split `NavRail` into stable server/client boundaries before more is added under it.\n",
